("s1","There is a book in a library in Connecticut that no one has been able to show how to read. Codebreakers, linguists and computers have worked on it for more than a century. This episode keeps three things apart: what is established about the book, what people have claimed, and what is still open."),
("s2","The object. It is catalogued as Beinecke M S 408, and it belongs to the Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library at Yale. About two hundred forty pages survive, out of an estimated two hundred seventy two, a figure reconstructed from gaps. It is handwritten on vellum, in a script Yale describes as unidentified, and illustrated with plants, star charts, and small figures that seem to be bathing. It is called the Voynich manuscript, after Wilfrid Voynich, the dealer who bought it in 1912.",
 "The object. It is catalogued as Bine-key M S 408, and it belongs to the Bine-key Rare Book and Manuscript Library at Yale. About two hundred forty pages survive, out of an estimated two hundred seventy two, a figure reconstructed from gaps. It is handwritten on vellum, in a script Yale describes as unidentified, and illustrated with plants, star charts, and small figures that seem to be bathing. It is called the Voy-nich manuscript, after Wilfrid Voy-nich, the dealer who bought it in 1912."),
("s3","Established: its age. In 2009, a team at the University of Arizona radiocarbon dated samples of the vellum and got a range of 1404 to 1438. That dates the animal skin, not the writing on it. Analyses of the ink and paints found materials consistent with the period. Together, these make a modern forgery difficult to argue."),
