("s5","Now the text, which is where the real puzzle lives. It runs to roughly one hundred seventy thousand characters, about thirty eight thousand transcribed words, with about eight thousand different forms. Counts vary between transcriptions, and a word here just means a string between spaces. The word frequencies follow a Zipf-like curve, the pattern found in natural languages, where a few words are very common and most are rare. Studies have also reported language-like patterns, such as related words clustering in related places. Patterns like these do not by themselves prove a language."),
("s6","But it also behaves unlike the languages we can test. Claire Bowern and Luke Lindemann reviewed the evidence in 2021. On one measure of letter predictability, conditional entropy, they report about two bits for the text, lower than in their samples of natural languages. Some characters appear almost only at the start or end of words. Scholars label two varieties of the text, A and B, with different profiles, after Prescott Currier. And Lisa Fagin Davis has argued, from the handwriting, that five scribes worked on it."),
("s7","Put those together and you have the central problem. To many researchers the text looks structured, yet no proposed reading has been broadly accepted. That describes the data. It is not an explanation."),
